Secretary Clinton welcome and we're here  at Swansea University where at the law  school with your name on it and you're  here to welcome the inaugural group of  Hillary Clinton global challenge  scholars so we're a long way from New  York or Washington how did this  relationship come about and it was the  most wonderful serendipity I received a  letter in the spring of 2017 asking if I  would be interested in an affiliation  with the University I am part Welsh  about 1/3 is my ancestry and there were  people here at the law school who had  followed some work that I had done for  many years and they asked if they could  name the law school for me I was  immensely honored I began talking with  them I came here this is my fourth visit  to Swansea and I just fell in love with  not only the area but also what the  University and the law school were doing  so I could not be happier and one of the  global challenges identified by this  scholarship with your name on it is  cyber security and you've talked a lot  about suspected Russian interference in  the 2016 US presidential election how  concerned are you about the next one I'm  very concerned and it's not just my  personal concern although that certainly  motivates me to try to learn as much as  I can it's what our own intelligence  officials under the current Trump  administration are also saying that  Russia never left our systems they are  involved in propaganda that they produce  and provide to Americans  disguised as though it were legitimate  news they are very much behind a lot of  what are called BOTS that flood the  internet and social media sites to carry  messages for or against candidates or  causes so I think that we haven't taken  the threat seriously and it's way past  time that we do and of course the UK is  about to head into a general election do  you have concerns around that well I  think there has been an accumulating  body of evidence that  there was Russian involvement in the  first brexit referendum and I think all  of us whether you are for or against the  current administration in the United  States for or against brexit should be  worried about an external power trying  to influence the votes of citizens and  as I said in London in a series of  interviews there is a government report  that should be released before the  election that does detail whatever the  Russian influence might have been  because I think voters have to wake up  and understand that it is an issue that  doesn't necessarily mean that they will  vote for or against somebody because of  it but don't we want to be informed  citizens before we cast votes I think we  do and on the subject of the election  Facebook has refused to ban political  advertising unlike Twitter so who and  your views got it right twitter has it  right because Facebook has refused to  take down blatantly false advertising  and what Twitter decided was look we  can't be the judge of it that would be  you know too much of a burden on us so  let's just ban political advertising I  think that is the right decision I'm  very disappointed in Facebook in the  United States slightly more than half of  the people get their news from Facebook  and in many instances it's the only  source of news so if they are being  bombarded with absolutely false attacks  on a political candidate how are they to  know any different because the  traditional media the broadcast and  cable networks refused to run the very  same ad that Facebook decided to run I  think that's another issue that we're  going to have to address in both of our  countries  Facebook says it's about free expression  and that all posts by politicians would  be treated as newsworthy content that  should be seen and heard well they are  misinterpreting as many experts have  told them what free expression means you  know free expression doesn't mean  yelling fire in a crowded theater free  expression does not mean knowingly  permitting false information on public  channels that has always been the the  rules that we have lived by in the  United States and you know Facebook is  basically saying you know we're totally  hands  we don't care what is said yet the  responsibility for conveying that  information has to rest somewhere and I  think if they will not exercise any  self-discipline there will have to be  regulatory and legal changes you've been  in UK promoting the book that you've  written with your daughter Chelsea the  book of gutsy women which celebrates  resilient and courageous women that we  should aspire to but at the moment for  politicians at least it's a time of  unprecedented abuse so many have come  out and talked about how they've had  threats made against them he'd have to  be really gutsy to want to be a  politician at the moment wouldn't you  unfortunately yes  I was really shocked when I was in  London talking with and and hearing from  former and current members of the House  of Commons the House of Lords about the  threats that they receive online the  hate that is directed toward them  unfortunately and sadly we can remember  the politically motivated murder of Joe  Cox a parliamentarian and a lot of women  are saying I want to serve but I can't  take the risk not just to themselves but  to their families because in some of the  instances those who were hurling those  threats were mentioning their children  it's a terrible loss for our democracy  we need to be encouraging people to run  for office and to serve and we need a  diversity of voices because that's what  a parliament should represent so I think  that the government law enforcement  everyone needs to step up and say that  they're not going to permit these kinds  of you know threats and they're  certainly going to provide security to  everyone who does run and serve I'm  talking of running for office you've  been asked a number of times about  whether you're considering running again  you haven't said yes but you have said  never say never so what is it that  you're weighing up here what's under  consideration what would swing it well I  never really seriously considered it but  I have been the subject of a number of  people urging me to do so I have no  plans to do it I  that there are many roles to play many  positions to champion that I'm going to  continue to pursue and I feel like it's  not just about individual candidates  anymore it's about our whole system of  democracy that is under attack and I  think we need a lot more voices saying  ok regardless of party regardless of  position we've got to protect the  fundamentals of democracy you know the  rule of law respect for our institutions  civil discourse minority rights these  are all things along with freedom of  speech and assembly and the like that  have built our two democracies you're  the oldest Parliament in the world we  have been at it for you know somewhat  over 200 years but now I think we face  threats from within and without and I am  going to continue to be very vocal about  that Anu and your daughter Chelsea are  celebrating women who are courageous and  resilient but are also changing the  world for others so would you like  Chelsea to run for president oh I don't  know I want her to do what makes her  happy and gives her purpose in life  she's an incredible spokesperson and  advocate on all kinds of causes and I  think she's very very gutsy herself but  any future political decision she  certainly is not thinking about it now  would have to be you know considered and  made by her but you think she could do  it in future I think she could do  anything I think she is an  extraordinarily talented person well  you've been here in the UK the first  public impeachment hearings have been  going on in the United States President  Trump has said he's been too busy to  watch have you been watching I've caught  excerpts of it because I had that time  differences made it a little bit  difficult because of my obligations and  I've read all of the reporting and  analysis and I think that the testimony  that was already presented is  devastating these are not partisan  people at all they are public servants  two very experienced diplomats who were  just reciting the facts as they knew  them what they had seen what they had  heard and why it mattered  look I have a lot of experience in  practicing law evaluating facts  understanding how it applies and I was  actually a young lawyer on the  impeachment staff that investigated  Richard Nixon when he was being  considered for impeachment and I think  that the Democrats in Congress who are  running this process have been very  careful vigilant about not getting ahead  of themselves following the facts where  they lead and I will not be at all  surprised if the house decides to  impeach Donald Trump so you think it  could well happen  I do because I know from the experience  with Richard Nixon the abuse of power  the obstruction of justice the contempt  of Congress that were the allegations  against him we've already seen evidence  of it's pretty simple you know the our  Constitution talks about not only high  crimes and misdemeanors but also bribery  and bribery doesn't have to just be  person-to-person it can be nation to  nation in the sense that a president  would abuse his office to try to coerce  the president of another country in  desperate need of military assistance to  protect his country against Russian  aggression that he would not get that  assistance unless he manufactured  information that could be used against  the president's political opponents that  is exactly the kind of abuse that the  people who wrote our Constitution were  most worried about I want to ask you  about a case here in Britain of harried  an young man 19 year old man he was  killed in a road accident and the  suspect who's the wife of a US diplomat  working here in the UK claims a  traumatic immunity and went back to the  United States he was secretary of state  for a number of years does that sound  like a proper use of diplomatic immunity  to you I first of all just want to  express you know my condolences to his  family I can't even imagine the pain and  suffering that they are feeling  the loss of their son and I also regret  the incident at the White House which  seemed quite clumsy and and and  heavy-handed that they were subjected to  there is a proper approach to  determining what is the appropriate use  of diplomatic immunity I think that  should certainly be looked into and our  government should be cooperating with  the authorities here in the UK to  determine whether or not it was there  are clear lines that govern diplomatic  immunity sometimes it causes  consternation we've had that in our  country when something happened and and  a diplomat was able to leave but then  there are grey areas so I would hope  that experienced heads are trying to  figure out the appropriate response and  do you think that the suspects unsecure  should be sent back to the UK to face  justice I can't jump to that conclusion  you know there's something called the  Vienna Convention there's all kinds of  precedent about when diplomatic immunity  applies when it doesn't that's why there  should be this very thoughtful inquiry  that our country and yours undertakes to  determine was it or was it not  appropriate and what should be the  follow-on remedies and actions depending  upon the finding and you'll be heading  back to the United States soon will you  be watching the general election unfold  in the UK closely very closely you know  I do not in any way take a stand in the  politics of the UK that is up to the  people here but it really matters  how the UK moves forward into the future  because you know you really are our very  special partner and when you believe as  I do that our democracies are under  attack from both forces within and  forces without we've got to get our acts  together and making a decision whatever  the outcome going forward or not remain  or not at least we've got to get some  closure so that decisions can be made  there is so much that we have to contend  with in the world today and there are so  many opportunities that speaking from my  own country we are letting go on address  you know we're not dealing with  technology and some of the questions  that it raises including the very real  set of issues around artificial  intelligence we are certainly not  dealing with climate change in the sense  of the emergency that it presents we're  not dealing with the refugee crisis you  know we have tens and hundreds of  thousands of people at our border Europe  has been you know really deluge by  desperate people what are we gonna do  about that how do we help people stay  where they are and have some kind of  future there so that they don't risk  their lives and also cause all kinds of  issues for us and the list goes on how  do we get the economy to work so that  middle class people feel like they're  getting a fair shake again and people  feel like you know when you say you can  make what you will of yourself you know  it's true it's not just a slogan so  right now I think in your country and  mine we are all bollixed up in politics  out of the worst kind we are you know  not coming together not finding common  ground not making good decisions  so elections should be always about the  future not looking backwards and and I  hope that you know within a year's time  both of our countries will be back on  the right track together and just to  revisit impeachment just for a moment  the impeachment hearings President Trump  calls it a witch-hunt President Trump  seems not to either understand or care  about the law rule of law or our  Constitution and he denies reality on a  daily basis it's how he communicates  what he wants people to believe and so  when evidence comes forward as it did  from very credible sources that he was  using his office to withhold military  aid that had been voted for by our  Congress in order to force a country's  resident to do his bidding that's not a  witch hunt those are troubling facts  that need to be investigated that's what  is going on now and sadly he seems to  only care about his own future not the  future of our democracy or our country  and it's exactly the kind of set of  facts that our founders who wrote our  constitution we're worried about you  know we have elections and they could  have been sitting around at the  Constitutional Convention saying okay so  we get a you know a bad character in and  he's doing things that are undermining  our national security and you know  enriching himself but we'll wait to the  next election but they didn't say that  they said you know when it's severe  enough we're gonna put a provision in  our Constitution that can be triggered  by an equal but separate branch the  Congress to determine whether a  president has defied the Constitution  defied the laws that's what the  impeachment process is for the way it  works is that the House of  Representatives considers the evidence  and then it's like an indictment or a  charge in a in a case and they decide  are there articles of impeachment to be  drawn or not then it goes to the Senate  for a trial and the Senate then actually  weighs the evidence they act in effect  as jurors it's not a witch hunt it's a  it's a it's a triggering of our  Constitution I don't know anyone who's  happy about it and even people like  myself who are very worried about his  presidency and how he's conducted  himself  I don't celebrate the fact that he has  triggered this impeachment investigation  it's not good for our country it's a  terrible remedy that is being deployed  but it's based on facts and it's based  on the law and it's based on the  Constitution so as many times as he said  it's a you know witch hunt he's wrong  and at some point he should accept  responsibility for what he said and what  he did

